> I'm not surprised – as this replaced the existing behaviour.

> We need a way to distinguish the platform. There is an argument for simple and just use MACOS_ENABLED, which is arguably no different from actual runtime conditional logic (but more efficient).

I don't think that we need to distinguish the platform. That would mean that every user implementing custom controls would have to do the same per-platform adjustments (e.g. for Wayland or Android). I think that we should compensate as many differences as possible in each respective `DisplayServer`, even through hacks if necessary.

From my understanding, there are two main implementations for smooth scrolling: fractions of a detent and screen coordinates:

 - Fractions of a detent can already be mapped through the `factor` property of `InputEventMouseButton`, so that's already perfectly universal IMO.

 - Screen coordinates are instead used on touchscreens and fancier (read: modern) touchpads, which are effectively tiny digitizers FWICT.

They're obviously both subject to taste so there will always be the need for user adjustment, both on the platform side and on the engine side, but the baseline is pretty clear to me.

From my research, at least MacOS, Android, and Wayland expose in some way screen-coordinates. *All* other platforms expose at least fractional wheels if not even fancier screen-space ones, with some more specific APIs. I propose to map `InputEventPanGesture` to screen-space events and any other implementations (which, for other platforms, is not even there yet mind you) to `InputEventMouseButton` with fractional wheel detents.

As a matter of fact, Wayland has similar sensitivity issues, yet the returned values seem to be pretty accurate when printing them. This patch (without this PR, and with #116516 for HiDPI users) makes tree scrolling work 1-to-1 on my main laptop, as if it were a touch-screen (although I don't have OS-emitted inertial scrolling):

```patch
diff --git a/scene/gui/tree.cpp b/scene/gui/tree.cpp
index d41fc65eb1..f919909621 100644
--- a/scene/gui/tree.cpp
+++ b/scene/gui/tree.cpp
@@ -4315,7 +4315,7 @@ void Tree::gui_input(const Ref<InputEvent> &p_event) {
 	Ref<InputEventPanGesture> pan_gesture = p_event;
 	if (pan_gesture.is_valid()) {
 		double prev_v = v_scroll->get_value();
-		v_scroll->set_value(v_scroll->get_value() + v_scroll->get_page() * pan_gesture->get_delta().y / 8);
+		v_scroll->set_value(v_scroll->get_value() + get_size().height * pan_gesture->get_delta().y / v_scroll->get_max());
 
 		double prev_h = h_scroll->get_value();
 		if (is_layout_rtl()) {
```

(Quickly hacked, the logic behind this is this proportion: `control_height : vscroll_max = pan_delta : vscroll_delta` , where `vscroll_delta` is the unknown in this case)

Hope this clears up my reasoning behind my proposal. Please let me know what you think.

> I did some reading on Windows and Linux trackpad events vary. Linux is even more complicated as different window managers and input systems will also affect how input events from a trackpad are produced 😭

@stuartcarnie On Windows, I believe trackpad events are just sent as regular mousewheel scrolling events and are handled as such in Godot. Linux on X11 also does not have trackpad events and behaves the same as Windows. Linux on Wayland functions very similarly to MacOS, and should always behave consistently across window managers because the input protocols are standardized (in my experience with switching between window managers, this is true).

I do wonder why this PR negates the 0.03 factor for MacOS trackpad deltas by adding a factor of 32, rather than removing both factors altogether. Removing them could potentially fix the issues with Linux, very similar to my attempted Linux solution in #116516, although I did find while testing my PR that Linux needed an additional factor of about 5 to feel like the scrolling speed was consistent with other applications--I'm unsure why.

@adamscott

> We could just expose this property (there's an equivalent for Wayland from what I saw) without touching the data itself.

Yea Wayland has [wl_pointer.axis_relative_direction](https://wayland.app/protocols/wayland#wl_pointer:enum:axis_relative_direction)
